ity ?payload_unit)). It does not mention origin_node_occupied. So origin_node_occupied remains true after unload? Actually load_origin_payload sets origin_node_occupied true, and does not unset it. unload_origin_payload does not affect origin_node_occupied, so it would stay true. So you could have both origin_node_occupied and origin_node_ready true after unload? Let's see: load sets occupied true, ready unchanged (still false? Actually load does not affect ready). activate sets ready true, does not affect occupied. So after activate, ready true, occupied false. After load, occupied true, ready still true? Wait load does not affect ready, so ready remains as previously set. If we activated (ready true) then load (occupied true) we get both ready and occupied true. Then unload sets ready true (already true) and availability confirmed, and frees cargo, but does not affect occupied. So after unload, occupied remains true? Actually load set occupied true, unload does not unset occupied, so occupied stays true. That seems odd but maybe okay.

